demo-zoom self-test
  ok a valid plan parses
  ok size and rate have defaults
  ok a malformed plan names the parse error (said: plan is not valid JSON: Expected property name or '}' in JSON at position 2 (line 1 column 3))
  ok rendering over the source is refused (said: source and output must differ; rendering over the input would destroy it)
  ok a path that would smuggle an option is refused (said: source may not start with "-" (it would be read as an ffmpeg option))
  ok a missing focus list is refused rather than assumed (said: plan needs a "focus" array (it may be empty))
  ok a segment that ends before it starts is refused (said: focus[0].end must be greater than start)
  ok a focus point outside the frame is refused (said: focus[0].x and .y are fractions of the frame and must be within 0..1)
  ok a zoom that shrinks the frame is refused (said: focus[0].zoom may not be below 1; 1 means no zoom)
  ok an ease too long for its segment is refused, with the reason (said: focus[0].ease of 0.9s does not fit twice inside a 1.00s segment; the zoom would never reach 2x)
  ok overlapping segments are refused (said: focus segments overlap (0-5 and 3-8); the camera cannot be in two places at once)
  ok an odd output size is refused before the encoder rejects it (said: size 1281x721 must have even dimensions; most encoders reject odd ones)
  ok a zero frame rate is refused (said: fps must be between 1 and 240)
  ok the camera is untouched before a segment begins
  ok the camera is released after a segment ends
  ok weight is zero exactly at both edges, so there is no jump
  ok weight reaches a full 1 in the middle
  ok zoom reaches the requested factor
  ok zoom rests at 1 outside every segment
  ok the ramp is monotonic, so the zoom never stutters
  ok the frame centres on the focus point at full zoom
  ok the frame is centred when nothing is in focus
  ok the zoom curve is continuous everywhere (largest step 0.0450)
  ok the rate is forced before the zoom, so the filter clock matches real time
  ok the frame is fitted to the declared size, so a coordinate means the same thing on any source
  ok pixels are square before the crop, so a non-square source cannot skew the zoom
  ok the zoom reads from a frame twice the output size, halving the smallest crop step
  ok the output size is still the declared one
  ok the zoom is a single pass, so there are no seams to resynchronise
  ok the crop origin is not coarsened to even pixels, which would enlarge each step rather than smooth it
  ok one output frame per input frame
  ok the output size is pinned in the filter
  ok the crop origin is clamped, so a focus point near an edge cannot show padding
  ok the expression is a sum of weights rather than nested conditionals
  ok time is derived from the frame index, the portable clock
  ok where two hard-cut segments meet, only the second is active (a 2x and a 3x do not add to 4x)
  ok a hard cut holds its own factor for its own span
  ok the last segment releases at its end rather than one frame late
  ok the interval is half-open in the emitted expression too, not only in the model
  ok a plan whose filter would exceed what a command line can carry is refused with the reason, not left to fail inside spawn
  ok a point near an edge lands where the crop can actually reach
  ok a point with room to spare lands exactly where it was asked to
  ok the explanation shows where an edge shot really lands, instead of echoing the request back
  ok the explanation says plainly that a shot was clamped
  ok an unknown source duration is declared rather than assumed fine
  ok a segment running past the end of the source is called out
  ok a segment inside the source is not flagged
  ok without a duration nothing is claimed about overrun either way
  ok a plan with no focus segments does not pretend to zoom
  ok a plan with no focus segments still normalises rate and size
  ok the grid seeks to the requested time
  ok the grid rules the frame into tenths, matching the plan coordinates
  ok the grid is drawn on the same fitted frame the render produces, so measured coordinates transfer
  ok the grid follows the declared output size rather than assuming one
  ok a negative grid time is refused
  ok ffmpeg is given an argv array, never a shell string
  ok the output path is the final argument
  ok audio is carried through when present and not demanded when absent
  ok audio is re-encoded rather than copied, which would fail on a container the codec cannot enter
  ok a source with no audio stream is given no audio encoder options, which would otherwise print an unused-AVOption warning
  ok a silent source still tolerates audio appearing, rather than refusing it
  ok a screenplay and a take compile into one segment per emphasised step
  ok the compiled plan renders the recording the take actually produced
  ok a compiled plan is stamped with the take it came from, so it cannot silently render against another recording
  ok the output frame and rate come from the take, not from a guess
  ok a segment starts a declared lead before the measured action and holds after it
  ok the leading anchor keeps the left edge of the field in frame (visible from 0.042, field starts 0.062)
  ok the leading anchor sits left of the field centre, which is where the text actually is
  ok a click is centred on the rectangle it clicked
  ok compiled segments never overlap, so the camera is never asked to be in two places
  ok a compiled plan passes the same validation as a hand-written one
  ok overlapping lead-in and hold are split rather than refused, and the split is reported
  ok the split result is still renderable
  ok a step the take recorded as failed is not zoomed into, because magnifying a mistake is worse than not zooming
  ok an emphasised step the take never recorded is named, not silently dropped
  ok a review row samples four frames
  ok the first cell shows the source just before the camera moves
  ok the source and the render are sampled at the same instant, so the pair shows what the zoom did to it
  ok a segment at the very start does not sample a negative time
  ok the legend refuses to let a contact sheet stand in for grounding
  ok a path with a space survives --print as one argument
  ok the filter is quoted in --print, so a shell cannot eat its parentheses
  ok an ordinary path is not needlessly quoted
  ok a zero-sized output is refused rather than failing inside the encoder (said: size 00x00 is too small to encode; use at least 16x16)
  ok a frame rate that would round to zero is refused (said: fps must be between 1 and 240)
  ok a quality setting libx264 would reject is refused here, where the message is readable (said: crf must be within 0..51, the range libx264 accepts)
  ok the camera fully releases between two segments
  ok the second segment reaches its own factor independently
  ok the explanation counts what will actually be rendered
demo-zoom self-test: 89 checks passed
